module ECL_UNITS.

import ECL_ERRORS.

context Units.
context Nesting.

> This model is normative for the lifecycle of a general ECL unit; its ordered
> entry and body values; successful completion with an ordered result stack;
> and failure with an error and restoration of the entry stack.
>
> It deliberately abstracts over evaluation steps, name resolution, concrete
> values and error dictionaries, environment writes, I/O, randomness, and all
> other non-stack effects. The surrounding language specification governs
> those subjects and requires that unit failure does not roll back effects
> already performed. The model also omits launch and observation policy, task
> identity, task trees, scheduling, awaiting, deadlines, cancellation causes,
> and reclamation. In particular, cancellation reaches this model only as an
> evaluator-supplied failure error, while a waiting operation's timeout does
> not alter the target unit.
>
> `@attempt` begins a unit, waits synchronously for one terminal transition,
> and reifies that outcome. `@spawn` begins the same kind of unit and returns a
> task capability immediately; `task.await` later reifies the attached unit's
> terminal outcome through the same mapping. The two therefore agree on entry
> stack, result envelope, and failure mapping. They differ in ownership:
> `@spawn` creates a task, which owns the tasks spawned inside it, while
> `@attempt` is a stack transaction that owns nothing, so tasks spawned inside
> it belong to the enclosing task or the session root. Task lifetime, ownership,
> and waiting behavior are governed by the concurrency model.
>
> `Begin unit` receives the complete entry-stack list and exact quotation body
> as separate ECL list values. It selects one never-before-created unit identity, records
> those inputs unchanged, and makes that unit active. Submissions and unit
> constructors differ in how they supply these inputs
> and expose the eventual outcome; those policies do not change unit execution
> semantics. `Begin unit` starts a unit with no parent: a submission's or a
> task's. Units that running code opens inside another, such as `@attempt`,
> `@load`, and module construction, begin through `Begin nested unit`.
Units ~> Begin unit @ entry: Value, body: Value, value-type entry = list-type, value-type body = list-type.
---
> Beginning a unit selects one never-before-created identity, records its exact
> entry stack and body, and makes it active with the entry stack installed.
some unit: Unit, ~unit-created? unit |
  unit-created?' unit and
  unit-active?' unit and
  ~unit-succeeded?' unit and
  ~unit-failed?' unit and
  unit-entry' unit = entry and
  unit-body' unit = body and
  unit-stack' unit = entry and
  unit-error' unit = unit-error unit and
  (all other: Unit, other != unit |
    unit-created?' other = unit-created? other and
    unit-active?' other = unit-active? other and
    unit-succeeded?' other = unit-succeeded? other and
    unit-failed?' other = unit-failed? other and
    unit-entry' other = unit-entry other and
    unit-body' other = unit-body other and
    unit-stack' other = unit-stack other and
    unit-error' other = unit-error other).

where

> A created unit is in exactly one phase: active, successfully completed, or
> failed. Entry and body remain fixed after creation. `unit-stack` denotes the
> stack at the modeled boundary: it begins as the entry stack and becomes the
> evaluator-supplied result on success. Intermediate evaluator stacks are
> outside the abstraction.
Unit.
{Units} unit-created? unit: Unit => Bool.
{Units} unit-active? unit: Unit => Bool.
{Units} unit-succeeded? unit: Unit => Bool.
{Units} unit-failed? unit: Unit => Bool.
{Units} unit-entry unit: Unit => Value.
{Units} unit-body unit: Unit => Value.
{Units} unit-stack unit: Unit => Value.
{Units} unit-error unit: Unit => Value.
> Nesting is fixed when a unit begins. A unit without a parent is a
> submission's or a task's; its failure is observed by the Session or through
> `task.await`, never by another unit.
{Nesting} unit-nested? unit: Unit => Bool.
{Nesting} unit-parent unit: Unit => Unit.
{Nesting} unit-reifying? unit: Unit => Bool.
---
> An active unit is created and is neither successful nor failed.
all candidate: Unit, unit-active? candidate |
  unit-created? candidate and
  ~unit-succeeded? candidate and
  ~unit-failed? candidate.

> A successfully completed unit is created and is no longer active or failed.
all candidate: Unit, unit-succeeded? candidate |
  unit-created? candidate and
  ~unit-active? candidate and
  ~unit-failed? candidate.

> A failed unit is created and terminal, carries an error value, and exposes
> its restored entry stack rather than the evaluator's partial stack.
all candidate: Unit, unit-failed? candidate |
  unit-created? candidate and
  ~unit-active? candidate and
  ~unit-succeeded? candidate and
  unit-stack candidate = unit-entry candidate and
  error-value? (unit-error candidate).

> Every created unit is in exactly one of the three lifecycle phases.
all candidate: Unit, unit-created? candidate |
  unit-active? candidate or
  unit-succeeded? candidate or
  unit-failed? candidate.

> Every created unit retains list values for its entry stack, quotation body,
> and exposed stack.
all candidate: Unit, unit-created? candidate |
  value-type (unit-entry candidate) = list-type and
  value-type (unit-body candidate) = list-type and
  value-type (unit-stack candidate) = list-type.

> Only a created unit is nested.
all candidate: Unit, ~unit-created? candidate | ~unit-nested? candidate.

> A nested unit's parent is another created unit.
all candidate: Unit, unit-created? candidate, unit-nested? candidate |
  unit-created? (unit-parent candidate) and
  unit-parent candidate != candidate.

> Units nest strictly: the parent of an active nested unit is active.
all candidate: Unit, unit-active? candidate, unit-nested? candidate |
  unit-active? (unit-parent candidate).

> A parent succeeds only if every failure nested in it was reified and was
> not a cancellation.
all candidate: Unit, unit-nested? candidate, unit-failed? candidate, unit-succeeded? (unit-parent candidate) |
  unit-reifying? candidate and
  ~cancelled-error? (unit-error candidate).

> Initially no unit is nested.
initially all candidate: Unit | ~unit-nested? candidate.

> Initially no unit identity has been created or entered a lifecycle phase.
initially all candidate: Unit |
  ~unit-created? candidate and
  ~unit-active? candidate and
  ~unit-succeeded? candidate and
  ~unit-failed? candidate.

where

> `Complete unit successfully` accepts the result produced by the omitted
> evaluator, makes the active unit terminal, and retains that result as the
> unit's terminal stack. The evaluator supplies the result; it is not chosen by
> the ECL operation that launched the unit.
Units ~> Complete unit successfully @ unit: Unit, result: Value, unit-created? unit, unit-active? unit, value-type result = list-type, (all child: Unit, unit-nested? child, unit-parent child = unit | ~unit-active? child and (~unit-failed? child or (unit-reifying? child and ~cancelled-error? (unit-error child)))).
---
> Successful completion makes the unit terminal and exposes the evaluator's
> complete result stack without changing its entry stack or body.
unit-created?' unit.
~unit-active?' unit.
unit-succeeded?' unit.
~unit-failed?' unit.
unit-entry' unit = unit-entry unit.
unit-body' unit = unit-body unit.
unit-stack' unit = result.
unit-error' unit = unit-error unit.

all other: Unit, other != unit |
  unit-created?' other = unit-created? other and
  unit-active?' other = unit-active? other and
  unit-succeeded?' other = unit-succeeded? other and
  unit-failed?' other = unit-failed? other and
  unit-entry' other = unit-entry other and
  unit-body' other = unit-body other and
  unit-stack' other = unit-stack other and
  unit-error' other = unit-error other.

where

> `Complete unit with failure` accepts the error produced by the evaluator or
> by a facility such as concurrency cancellation. Errors are ordinary ECL
> values satisfying the imported `error-value?` schema. Completion makes
> the active unit terminal, records the error value, and restores the complete
> entry stack. The
> evaluator's partial stack is neither retained nor exposed. Every error kind
> follows this same unit transition; causes and concrete error data are outside
> the model.
Units ~> Complete unit with failure @ unit: Unit, error: Value, unit-created? unit, unit-active? unit, error-value? error, (all child: Unit, unit-nested? child, unit-parent child = unit | ~unit-active? child).
---
> Failure makes the unit terminal, records the evaluator-supplied error, and
> restores the complete entry stack. The partial evaluator stack is not
> retained or exposed.
unit-created?' unit.
~unit-active?' unit.
~unit-succeeded?' unit.
unit-failed?' unit.
unit-entry' unit = unit-entry unit.
unit-body' unit = unit-body unit.
unit-stack' unit = unit-entry unit.
unit-error' unit = error.

all other: Unit, other != unit |
  unit-created?' other = unit-created? other and
  unit-active?' other = unit-active? other and
  unit-succeeded?' other = unit-succeeded? other and
  unit-failed?' other = unit-failed? other and
  unit-entry' other = unit-entry other and
  unit-body' other = unit-body other and
  unit-stack' other = unit-stack other and
  unit-error' other = unit-error other.

where

> `Begin nested unit` opens a unit inside an active parent unit. Units nest
> strictly: a parent cannot become terminal while a unit nested in it is
> active. A reifying nested unit, as `@attempt` opens, turns its failure into
> data its parent continues with. Any other nested failure propagates, so its
> parent cannot succeed. Cancellation is never reified: a nested unit failed
> by a cancellation error prevents its parent's success whatever boundary
> opened it, so cancellation fails every unit of the cancelled task or root
> from the innermost out.
Units, Nesting ~> Begin nested unit @ parent: Unit, entry: Value, body: Value, reifying: Bool, unit-created? parent, unit-active? parent, value-type entry = list-type, value-type body = list-type.
---
> Beginning a nested unit is beginning a unit that also records its parent.
> Whether its boundary reifies failure is the opener's choice and stays fixed.
some unit: Unit, ~unit-created? unit |
  unit-created?' unit and
  unit-active?' unit and
  ~unit-succeeded?' unit and
  ~unit-failed?' unit and
  unit-entry' unit = entry and
  unit-body' unit = body and
  unit-stack' unit = entry and
  unit-error' unit = unit-error unit and
  unit-nested?' unit and
  unit-parent' unit = parent and
  unit-reifying?' unit = reifying and
  (all other: Unit, other != unit |
    unit-created?' other = unit-created? other and
    unit-active?' other = unit-active? other and
    unit-succeeded?' other = unit-succeeded? other and
    unit-failed?' other = unit-failed? other and
    unit-entry' other = unit-entry other and
    unit-body' other = unit-body other and
    unit-stack' other = unit-stack other and
    unit-error' other = unit-error other and
    unit-nested?' other = unit-nested? other and
    unit-parent' other = unit-parent other and
    unit-reifying?' other = unit-reifying? other).
